--------------
 1 - Overview
--------------

PP16 is a fixed-frame pixel editor for making 64 by 64 pixel, 16-colour
sprite artwork.  A compact 320 by 200 Tandy graphics interface provides a
zoomed drawing canvas, an actual-size image preview, a palette, a clipboard
preview and the controls needed for precise pixel work.

The document is deliberately one frame and one size.  This keeps the editor
small enough for real-mode DOS while leaving a clear, direct workflow:

  1) Draw or edit the current 64 by 64 image.
  2) Use the selection and clipboard tools to reuse part of it.
  3) Save the finished frame as a native .P16 file.

PP16 uses the standard fixed Tandy 16-colour palette.  It does not provide
animation, a screen designer, variable-size images, palette editing or
image-wide special effects.  Those omissions are intentional: PP16 focuses
on consistent hand-edited sprite frames.


Importing prepared images (version 1.1)
---------------------------------------
Use File > Import and choose an indexed .PCX or PIXELplus 256 .PUT file in
the current directory. PCX must be version 5, 8-bit, one-plane RLE with its
256-entry RGB palette. Other PCX profiles and true-colour files are rejected.
The selected image must be at most 64x64. Smaller images go at the top left
with black padding. There are no resize or crop controls.

For PUT, choose an image using the < / > buttons or Up/Down. Tab changes
fields. Enter accepts and Escape cancels. The palette field starts with the
associated BASENAME.APF; you can type a different .APF or .PAL name. PP16
searches the current directory, PALETTES, then ..\PALETTES. A missing palette
must be supplied explicitly. Palettes contain 256 four-byte VGA DAC entries.

Conversion may take time on the target CPU. Escape cancels it. A successful
import is unnamed and unsaved; Save asks for a .P16 destination. Ctrl+Z
restores the previous pixels and dirty flag, while the image stays unnamed.
A failed or cancelled import preserves the current image, filename, undo,
clipboard and paint settings. Input and palette files are opened read-only.
The conversion keeps exact Tandy colours, gray neutrality and literal pixels;
it does not change your selected gradient paint. See docs/IMPORT.md for the
full supported profile, limits and development validation.


-------------------
 2 - PP16 Features
-------------------

PP16 includes the following features:

 * A fixed 64 by 64 pixel, 16-colour image using the standard Tandy palette.

 * A zoomed edit canvas at 2x, 4x or 8x, with an optional grid and a separate
   actual-size image preview.

 * Brush, bucket fill, line, circle and square drawing tools.  Brush size is
   shared by the brush and shape tools and can be set from 1 to 5 pixels.

 * Two-colour gradient/dither paint in exact 10% steps.  The permanent selector
   sits below the palette and the foreground swatch shows the active pattern.

 * Rectangle and ellipse selection, with optional Ctrl-constrained squares
   and circles.

 * A fixed-memory clipboard that can Copy, Cut, Paste, mirror, rotate and
   scale rectangular or elliptical selections.  Black is an ordinary opaque
   colour; it is never silently treated as transparent.

 * One completed-action Undo.  A stroke, shape, Cut, Paste or Clear action is
   undone as one operation.  Escape cancels an unfinished stroke or shape.

 * Native .P16 image files and .CLP clipboard files, each checked fully before
   loading.  Saves are written, reopened and verified before replacement.

 * File > Import converts prepared indexed PCX and PIXELplus 256 PUT images
   into literal Tandy pixels using exact-colour-preserving Floyd-Steinberg.
   Import is a single undoable action. See the import instructions below.

 * DOS 8.3 filename dialogs, recoverable overwrite handling and retained
   backups under the TRASH directory.

 * Keyboard support for common editing, zooming, grid, panning and clipboard
   operations, alongside the mouse-driven user interface.


-------------------------
 3 - System Requirements
-------------------------

PP16 is a 16-bit real-mode DOS program.  It is targeted at a Tandy 1000HX
with its 640 KiB conventional-memory layout and Tandy 16-colour graphics
hardware.  It has been built for an 8086-compatible processor; a NEC V20 is
appropriate for the target machine.

To run PP16, provide:

 * MS-DOS or a compatible DOS environment.

 * Tandy mode 09h, 320 by 200, 16-colour graphics support.  PP16 reserves the
   required upper graphics page before it changes video mode and refuses to
   run if that page is already in conflicting use.

 * A Microsoft-compatible mouse and a loaded mouse driver for normal editing.
   The editor's main tool, palette and menu workflow is mouse-oriented.

 * A writable current directory for .P16, .CLP and TRASH files.

PP16 does not require a GRAFIX TSR, ADJMEM, EMS or XMS.  The conventional-
memory baseline is intentional.  An emulator may be useful for development
and testing, but it is not a substitute for testing a particular physical
machine, mouse driver or DOS configuration.


---------------------------
 4 - Files and Directories
---------------------------

Keep the supplied program files together in the directory from which PP16 is
run.  The following files have these purposes:

File name       Use
--------------  -------------------------------------------------------------
PP16.EXE        The PP16 editor program.
PP16UI.P16      The required 64 by 64 toolbar-icon resource.  Keep this file
                beside PP16.EXE; it is not an editable drawing document.
PP16.DOC        This user information file.
README.TXT      Distribution installation and getting-started instructions.
HELP.TXT        Quick controls and troubleshooting for end users.
INSTALL.BAT     Distribution installer; run from the extracted package folder.
CREDITS.TXT     Author, reference and runtime credit notices.
*.P16           A native PP16 64 by 64 image.
*.CLP           A native PP16 clipboard image, rectangular or elliptical.

PP16 works in its current DOS directory.  Its file dialogs accept DOS 8.3
names and list .P16 and .CLP files found there.  They do not browse directories
or accept paths, wildcard names or DOS device names.

When a file is replaced, PP16 retains the former version rather than deleting
it.  Recovery copies and unsuccessful temporary writes are placed below:

    TRASH\YYYYMMDD\Snnnn

YYYYMMDD is the date and Snnnn is a numbered save operation.  Do not remove
these retained files unless you have made any copies you need.  PP16 does not
automatically empty the TRASH directory.


--------------------------------
 5 - PP16 Native File Formats
--------------------------------

PP16 has two native binary formats.  They are not Microsoft BASIC PUT files,
and a PP16 .CLP file does not claim compatibility with another program's file
of the same extension.

 * .P16 is one complete PP16 image.  PP16 currently accepts 64 by 64 images
   only.  Its payload is 2,048 bytes and its total file size is 2,068 bytes.

 * .CLP is one PP16 clipboard image.  Its width and height may each be from 1
   through 64 pixels.  It also records whether the clipboard is rectangular
   or elliptical.

Both formats use the same 20-byte header layout.  Multi-byte numbers are
unsigned little-endian values.  Pixel rows are written from top to bottom;
the even X pixel occupies the high nibble of each packed byte.

Offset  Bytes  Meaning
------  -----  --------------------------------------------------------------
  0       4    Magic: P16 plus DOS EOF marker (50 31 36 1A), or CLP plus
                DOS EOF marker (43 4C 50 1A).
  4       2    Format version: 1.
  6       2    Header size: 20 bytes.
  8       2    Width in pixels.
 10       2    Height in pixels.
 12       2    Flags: 0 for .P16 and rectangular .CLP files; 1 for an
                elliptical .CLP file.
 14       2    Palette identifier: 0, the fixed standard 16 Tandy colours.
 16       4    Packed payload length: ceiling(width / 2) multiplied by height.
 20       n    Packed pixel rows.

PP16 rejects unsupported versions, incorrect header sizes, dimensions, flags,
palette identifiers, lengths, extra trailing data and short reads before it
changes the open image or clipboard.  A failed read leaves the existing work
in place.


--------------------
 6 - Starting PP16
--------------------

At the DOS prompt, change to the directory containing PP16.EXE and the
PP16UI.P16 resource.  For example:

    CD \PP16
    PP16

The ready-to-run distribution includes INSTALL.BAT.  From its extracted
directory, type INSTALL to copy the program and user documentation into
C:\PP16, or INSTALL D:\PP16 to choose another DOS 8.3 destination.  Existing
package files are never overwritten.  See README.TXT before installation.

PP16 enters Tandy graphics mode after it has verified that the required video
memory is available.  If the memory protection check fails, PP16 returns to
DOS with a refusal message instead of drawing into memory owned by something
else.

Open an existing image from File / Open after PP16 has started.  PP16 does not
use a filename as a normal command-line argument.  File / Exit returns to DOS;
if image pixels have changed since the last save, PP16 asks whether to Cancel
or Discard the pending changes.  Cancel is the default choice.


----------------------
 7 - The PP16 Screen
----------------------

The PP16 screen is arranged for quick sprite work:

 * Tool panel -- at the upper left.  Select, Clipboard, Bucket Fill and Brush
   occupy the top row; Cut, Circle, Square and Line occupy the bottom row.

 * Brush-size controls -- below the tool panel.  The minus and plus buttons
   set the shared size used by Brush, Circle, Square and Line.

 * Colour panel -- at the lower left.  Left-click a palette colour to choose
   the foreground colour; right-click one to choose the background colour.
   The endpoint boxes and dither ramp below the palette select gradient paint.

 * Edit canvas -- the large central grid.  This is the zoomed view of the
   64 by 64 document and is where drawing and selections take place.

 * Zoom controls -- below the canvas.  Use the 2X, 4X and 8X buttons, or the
   matching number keys, to set the edit magnification.

 * Image preview -- at the upper right.  This shows the entire image at its
   true 64 by 64 size.  At 4X and 8X it includes a viewport outline.

 * Clipboard panel -- at the lower right.  It shows the current clipboard at
   its actual size, its dimensions, a CLR control and transform controls.

 * Status area -- below the canvas.  It reports document or cursor coordinates
   and reflects the current view.

Use F1 or Help / Quick Help for the short on-screen reference.  Help / About
PP16 shows the program and reference credits.


------------------------------------------------
 8 - Drawing, Selection and Clipboard Controls
------------------------------------------------

Drawing and colour
------------------

Use left-click or left-drag on the edit canvas to draw with the selected tool.
Right-click or right-drag on the canvas samples the foreground colour at the
point under the pointer.  Tab swaps solid foreground and background colours.
When gradient paint is active, Tab selects the solid background as foreground
and keeps the background colour unchanged.

Brush paints square marks at the selected size.  Bucket Fill replaces the
connected area under the pointer with the foreground colour.  Circle begins
at its centre, Square begins at its first corner, and Line begins at its first
endpoint.  These shape tools show a thin dashed grey guide while dragging.
Releasing the left button applies the colour, dither pattern and thickness as
one action.  Escape cancels an unfinished stroke or shape.

Right-click the Circle or Square tool button to toggle filled mode.  The label
shows FILLED CIRCLE or FILLED SQUARE.  Left-click selects the tool while keeping
its mode.  The primary paint draws the border at the selected size; the solid
secondary colour fills the interior, including black.  Each tool remembers its
mode for the session.  Both colours, size and mode are captured at mouse press.

To use gradient paint, click the left endpoint box below the palette, then
left-click its palette colour.  Repeat with the right endpoint box.  Click
along the ramp between the boxes to choose 0%, 10%, 20%, ... 100% of the right
endpoint colour.  A marker shows the chosen step; the foreground swatch shows
the dither pattern and percentage.  Escape cancels endpoint colour selection.

The mix uses a repeating 10 by 10 pixel tile with exact 10% increments.  It
stays anchored to image coordinates as strokes overlap or the canvas pans.
The pattern consists of ordinary pixels from the two chosen palette colours;
it does not add hardware colours.  A partial tile at an image edge can have a
different local ratio.  Endpoint colours and the selected step are session
controls; saved images contain the resulting pixels.

Brush, Line, Circle, Square, Bucket Fill and Clear to Colour all use the mix.
Shape guides show position; the mix appears on release.  An ordinary left
palette click or canvas eyedropper selects solid paint again, retaining the
endpoint settings.
Right palette clicks still set the solid background.  Copy, Cut, Paste, Open
and Save preserve literal image pixels rather than applying the active mix.

The Action menu provides Clear and Clear to Colour.  Clear fills the image
with black; Clear to Colour fills it with the foreground colour or mix.  Alt+C is
the keyboard shortcut for Clear.  Either clear action can be undone.

Selection and Cut
-----------------

Choose Select, then drag across the canvas to make an inclusive rectangular
selection.  Hold Ctrl while dragging to constrain it to a square.  Right-click
the Select toolbar button to switch between rectangular and elliptical
selection.  In ellipse mode, Ctrl constrains the drag box to a circle.

The grey marquee is only an on-screen overlay.  It does not alter pixels,
dirty state or Undo.  Selecting a different drawing tool removes the marquee
but leaves the clipboard unchanged.

Use Ctrl+C or Edit / Copy to copy the selection.  Use Ctrl+X, Edit / Cut, or
the Cut toolbar button to copy it and clear the selected pixels to the current
background colour.  Cut is one undoable document action.

Clipboard and Paste
-------------------

The Clipboard toolbar button, Ctrl+V or Edit / Paste starts floating placement
of the current clipboard.  The paste outline initially appears centred in the
visible canvas.  Move it with the mouse or arrow keys.  Left-click or press
Enter to commit the paste; press Escape to cancel it without changing pixels.
Pasted pixels clip safely at the document edge.

Edit / Paste Behind is a checked toggle.  When enabled, a source clipboard
pixel is written only where the destination pixel matches the current
background colour.  It does not make any clipboard colour transparent.

The Clipboard panel's red CLR button empties the session clipboard.  Its three
controls mirror the clipboard left-to-right, rotate it 90 degrees clockwise,
or scale it.  These controls affect only the clipboard, not the document or
Undo history.  For Scale, left-click adds one pixel to both dimensions and
right-click removes one, with each dimension limited to 1 through 64 pixels.

View and keyboard reference
----------------------------

Key or control             Action
-------------------------  -----------------------------------------------
2, 4 or 8                  Set canvas zoom to 2X, 4X or 8X.
F4                         Toggle the zoom grid.
Arrow keys                 Pan the zoomed view by one document pixel.
Shift + arrow keys         Pan by half the visible view.
Click or drag image preview Centre the zoomed view at that preview point.
F1                         Open Quick Help.
Ctrl+Z                     Undo the most recent completed document action.
Ctrl+C / Ctrl+X / Ctrl+V   Copy / Cut / begin Paste.
Tab                        Swap foreground and background colours.
Alt+C                      Clear the image to black.
Escape                     Cancel a current action, dialog or floating paste.

At 2X, the entire 64 by 64 image fits within the canvas, so panning has
no further range.  At 4X and 8X, use the arrow keys for fine movement or drag
the image preview for an immediate coarse move.


-------------------------------------
 9 - Saving, Opening and Recovery
-------------------------------------

File / New starts a blank black 64 by 64 image.  File / Save writes the
current .P16 file; an unnamed image opens the Save As dialog.  File / Save As
always asks for a filename.  If an extension is omitted, .P16 is added.

File / Open lists both .P16 and .CLP files.  Opening a .P16 image replaces the
document after complete validation.  If the document has unsaved pixels, PP16
first asks whether to Cancel or Discard.  Opening a .CLP file replaces only the
clipboard; it preserves the open image, view, filename, unsaved state and Undo.

To save a clipboard, choose File / Save As and enter a name ending in .CLP.
The clipboard's dimensions and rectangular or elliptical shape are retained.
An empty clipboard cannot be saved.

The filename dialog accepts typed DOS 8.3 names or a selected listed name.
Tab changes fields; arrow keys and Page Up/Page Down move through the list;
Home and End move within a field or to the list ends; Enter accepts and Escape
cancels.  Replacing a file requires confirmation and defaults to Cancel.

PP16 protects a successful replacement as follows:

  1) It writes a temporary file below TRASH for the current save operation.
  2) It closes, reopens and validates the temporary file, including every
     packed pixel byte.
  3) Only after validation does it move an existing target into that TRASH
     folder and rename the verified new file into place.

If a write, close, validation or rename fails, PP16 retains the current image
in memory and attempts to retain or restore the previous on-disk file.  Check
the reported error and the corresponding TRASH subfolder before retrying.


